{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties","id":1166651,"title":"Rage and the Curse versus Human Ties","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-11T01:00:47-08:00","id":1166651,"title":"Rage and the Curse versus Human Ties","content":"I have for a long time now noted that, that a lot of people often ignored the Curse and the Rage the Garou have in favour of being employed and having strong ties to humanity. I figured after Black Fox's Rage across New York retrospective, that I'd put up a thread discussing this issue.<br /> <br /> This might sound like a small gripe, but I think it has some long reaching implications, especially when it comes to kinfolk and the role they and garou play in their society. As well as pondering if the Curse should be used more like Primal-Urge is used in Werewolf the Forsaken.<br /> <br /> <br /> With the Kinfolk, as a long time forum and chat player, I have seen a particular type of kinfolk pop up. Often female, often young, often basically mere breeding stock for their garou mate. Now, I must note that these games are played 24/7 and so have the chance of playing out relationships in much more depth than in sessions. I have no problem with this. What I have an issue with, is that these kinfolk are often totally under the thumb of their garou mate and they often do not contribute to the sept much aside from some off-screen stuff.<br /> <br /> Now, Kinfolk are subservient to their garou, but the kicker in the books is that they also do a lot of work that the garou just cannot do because of the Curse. So when the garou are belittling them, they act like condescending dicks. To the chat/forum kinfolk, garou belittling and controlling them feels justified at times.<br /> <br /> I do think this behaviour from the players in these very old communities comes from the garou pcs wanting to interact with humanity a lot. Leaving the kinfolk pcs in a situation where they end up being limited. This is also a factor in some sourcebooks, where the Curse is ignored in favour of the NPC having a job or good ties to humanity. The newest MET book outright lists jobs that different garou of different auspices and tribes can do. In general, unless the ST particularly pushes for it, the Curse seems to be often ignored by the players and the writers alike.<br /> <br /> This brings to mind one question, is the Curse something that you guys would miss if it was removed? For me, I would like it to be used, but often my fellow players would not agree with this. The mentality I have seen is geared to garou that more often are more likely to do a lot of grunt work themselves and are less likely just to focus on spiritualism and fighting the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> In this, I must admit that a system similar to Forsaken's Primal-Urge would be a much better overall, allowing the PCs to interact and have stronger ties to humanity overall unless they choose to sacrifice that for more power. Given the mentality against the garou being moochers being so strong among the player base (from what I have noticed) and the heavy focus on taking part in human activities, this would work very well.<br /> <br /> Of course, this would further limit the KInfolk. But honestly, I think Kinfolk are much better as NPCs than PCs if the players and the STs aren't going to focus on their work.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T01:39:15-08:00","id":1166659,"title":"","content":"I think the thing is that the Curse isn't that bad at lower Rage. Even at Rage 4 (that's starting for a Galliard base) only inflicts the Curse on ~48% of the population. And Willpower is by no means evenly distributed (a Rage 4 school teacher is in a lot more trouble than a Rage 4 SWAT officer).<br /> <br /> I feel like part of the issue is that the core books and Kin books tend to emphasize the Curse beyond what the mechanics say, while most of the other supplements actually follow the mechanical implications (not always, there's lots of stupid things like high Rage school teachers in the books). Among Garou that don't bother to raise their Rage much (and this really does seem to be a divide in players; either you keep your Rage low and can generally interact with humanity, or you go for lots of Rage and accept the Curse and avoid people), holding a job is probably not such a hard thing to pull off. Rage 1-2 characters barely suffer the Curse at all. Rage 2-3 characters can easily hold down jobs as long as they avoid a few where you're going to deal with low Willpower people a lot (or if you do, it's not really going to matter), and Rage 3-4 you can probably swing a job if you're careful with what career you go into.<br /> <br /> The core book especially seems to expect you to buy up Rage for the significant power it means in combat and for Gifts, despite the drawbacks. Play experience tends to teach people to leave that to the Ahrouns and people that want to go very combat heavy. Which goes beyond the Curse, since there's also the Beast Within, and Frenzy.<br /> <br /> I would love to see the mechanics reflect the setting the game layouts out in the core book more, but it would probably need a pretty significant rethinking of the mechanics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T03:56:23-08:00","id":1166669,"title":"","content":"The most common case for this issue is that most story tellers don't create detailed septs that include both story background and detailed number population, of kinfolk, spirits and garou.<br /> A sept must include garou relatives both in kinfolk family names and notable wolf kinfolk individuals than make lupus garou seek them out for mates both male and female.<br /> Even though in kinfolk relatives the curse does not play any deal, High Rage does.<br /> From here lets extend a bit further towards the outside world that surrounds the sept, is it a big or a small city scape, do the kinfolk have social ties to that community, do garou have such ties ? How often ? How abou love mates ?<br /> Don't be ashamed to turn a session to romance novel once in a while, and play with the moon phases, and bang ! The curse slams the ground.<br /> What about garou with careers ? Even a Rage fueled garou that works as a model or waitress will have great trouble with colleagues and customers.<br /> <br /> - Excuse me sir your tea<br /> -Take the money and leave me alone b!^&amp;$<br /> -How did you call me ? Grrrrrr <br /> <br /> <br /> Something like that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12906-story-letter","name":"Story Letter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12906_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T14:00:39-08:00","id":1166806,"title":"","content":"The system still doesn't really support it.<br /> <br /> A Rage 2 Garou only inflicts the Curse on 10% of the population. So even if it would bite into tips, it wouldn't stop them from managing to get along with most people. The Beast Within doesn't kick in since they'll have at least Willpower 3. And no phase of the moon is going to really make Frenzy checks a problem.<br /> <br /> Does in semi-decent social traits, and the Persuasion Gift, and... the few times a year that Rage might cause a problem can be smoothed over just fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T14:18:52-08:00","id":1166811,"title":"","content":"I think the Curse is very important and emphasize it in my chronicles. It's one of the few things in the game that helps bolt the setting into the typical horror movies and stories we expect in the World of Darkness as opposed to the high fantasy adventure the game can too easily become. Establishing the status quo early on helps the PC incorporate their character into the setting appropriately and guides them in their future XP spending.<br /> <br /> I typically play tabletop so I don't have an issue with PC kinfolk. All kinfolk are NPCs whether they are GM characters or part of the Kinflk background. If there's issues, it is because the players don't adequately think out their character creation and don't buy the Background and become frustrated when they can't do something because they only have their Garou and no kinfolk to use.<br /> <br /> Sometimes that Garou PC does need to interact with humans for routine story purposes, and I allow PCs to spend Willpower to subdue the Rage within them to alleviate the Curse for a scene. When that happens, mortals are only affected as if they were kinfolk, and kinfolk don't notice anything at all. Since Willpower is a finite resource, this isn't something I've seen abused.<br /> <br /> I think when it comes to online games, a major problem in general is that players are looking for something to do. They want characters that can participate in a scene if they're interested. They don't want to sit back and do nothing because some other PC should be the one doing it. It can really kill the scene and enthusiasm for the game, if this or that PC isn't around so we can't advance. In similar circumstances, many plots involving the umbra are difficult because unless there is a theurge present, interaction with the spirits can be difficult. Sometimes games get around that by having spirits speak the Garou Tongue or the vernacular, but that ends up crapping over the theurges like how Ana described happens to kinfolk.<br /> <br /> The Curse should quickly cripple most Garou's ability to interact normally among the Flock. Rage 1 Garou are fine. Rage 2 Garou only affect 10% of the population, which is probably made up mostly of small children, the elderly, and the meekest adults. Most of those can be avoided on a regular basis, and the few that aren't will be seen as the odd ones to the Garou's normal social peers. These Garou can pass in human society without many problems. (In practice though, many Theurges will soon be acting in strange ways to humans as a result of their spirit interactions).<br /> <br /> Rage 3 Garou will start finding it to be very difficult. 30% of humanity is a large number, and now there are all sorts of people who need to be avoided. At that stage, there will be a noticeable enough amount of people actively avoiding the Garou out of fear that questions will be asked, police will show up, and normal social interactions can be poisoned (your coworker Bob used to like you, but once his wife met you and decided she hates you, he hasn't been as friendly). At that stage certain kind of jobs can still be done, provided social interaction is minimized. It also helps if the job in question allows both some privacy or isolation as well as attracting coworkers who would have high Willpower to begin with. These would be hard jobs in isolated places; but are also the kind of resource extraction jobs many Garou would find problematic. There would also be jobs involving illegal activities, private detectives, security, or other violence oriented jobs that the Garou could be selective about it. This couldn't include actual law enforcement though - police have to come into contact with too many regular people. Long term office jobs aren't feasible at this level. Certain kinds of third shift jobs that otherwise wouldn't work in normal business hours could work. So interaction is not impossible, but is limited. The Curse is affecting the character's life in a meaningful way.<br /> <br /> At Rage 4, the Garou would have to be making a living off the land by themselves, and hopefully with kinfolk helping them. This would be the mountain man hermit, or someone working on a family farm or ranch. In today's world, YouTube performer could probably also work. So would highly illegal activities like &quot;pscyho mob hitman&quot; or &quot;third world mercenary.&quot; The aforementioned Rage 4 SWAT member would probably be OK IF he just had to deal with his immediate coworkers, but he doesn't. I don't think he'd ever be able to reach that spot to begin with. I highly doubt he'd be able to get through a service academy, high school or college, the first few years on the beat, or even ordinary life without some kind of incident that kills his chance to get that. There would be why too many complaints about that &quot;maniac cop&quot; scaring half of the city, or general complaints that at the least would mark him as a poor team player. Psychologists evaluating him would mark down all kinds of things in his profile that would ruin his chances. Same thing for the military - he'd be washed out of boot camp as unfit unless we're talking Third World warlord militias.<br /> <br /> At Rage 5 and above, the Garou would not be able to hold down any job at all except in the most extraordinary or isolated of circumstances. Interaction with the Flock would be minimal. He'd be the crazed loner everyone avoids except a few high Willpower individuals he might have befriended, or who volunteers to see the guy when needed because everyone else is crapping their pants at the mention of the guy.<br /> <br /> Exceptions exist in small towns and villages (and organizations) that may be kinfolk only. In that case, maybe there is a local policeman who is Rage 5 that is effective at scaring away unwanted intruders. Or a bartender Galliard who likes talking to his customers. Or a community leader who is a Philodox in an isolated &quot;religious&quot; community similar to the Amish or Mennonites. Or the owner of a small business that employs only kinfolk, and is small enough to avoid the eyes of the government on their employment practices. But in all of those cases, these Garou would be isolated and sheltered from ordinary humanity. But even in kinfolk only towns, ordinary humans appear because kinfolk to kinfolk matches sometimes produce ordinary humans who would need to be removed at a certain time. And of course, at some point in time a regular human shows up in town because their car had a flat, or a government agency decides to audit, or a nosey reporter shows up for some reason.<br /> <br /> The Curse isn't a &quot;I don't like you feeling,&quot; it is a &quot;My life is in danger if I stay around you feeling.&quot; People can tolerate someone they don't like. They can't tolerate someone they feel danger from. It creates too much stress. Even among Kinfolk, the Curse isn't eliminated, it is just lessened. That gives the ST some leeway on how to interpret things for individual kinfolk and scenes, but the mass of kinfolk will still have a negative reaction the more Rage the Garou has.<br /> <br /> That is not a problem for me. It pushes the Garou into the wilderness, liminal spaces, and isolated locales that the horror genre puts werewolves in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T15:57:05-08:00","id":1166848,"title":"","content":"I agree. The Curse is an essential element of Werewolf. It is the consequence of the Impergium. The Garou violated their sacred responsibilities for their own arrogant, selfish reasons and the Curse is what resulted. Now in the modern day, the only way to avert the Apocalypse is to guide the Humans away from their current path -- which is now effectively all but an impossibility. Maybe the Gurahl could have done it, but the Garou decimated them. Maybe the Grondr or the Apis could have made the connection to the humans, but the Garou saw to them as well...<br /> <br /> If you get rid of the Curse, you aren't playing werewolves anymore... you're playing the X-Men or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T17:08:03-08:00","id":1166856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Uniform Two Six</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166848#post1166848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree. The Curse is an essential element of Werewolf. It is the consequence of the Impergium. The Garou violated their sacred responsibilities for their own arrogant, selfish reasons and the Curse is what resulted. Now in the modern day, the only way to avert the Apocalypse is to guide the Humans away from their current path -- which is now effectively all but an impossibility. Maybe the Gurahl could have done it, but the Garou decimated them. Maybe the Grondr or the Apis could have made the connection to the humans, but the Garou saw to them as well...<br /> <br /> If you get rid of the Curse, you aren't playing werewolves anymore... you're playing the X-Men or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>Still, just from the reactions of other players and the general mentality I have seen about this even from some of the writers, people don't want that isolation. They want their characters to be EMT workers, police officers, doctors and so on. I guess it is also the aging of the playerbase as a factor, too. In the first WtA game I officially played, I got told that my Rage 4 Galliard should find her own apartment and job in the city, garou do not live in communes.<br /> <br /> Plus all the concepts I have seen around, from Rank 5 caerns with no live in members to the various military personel ahrouns, everything is geared towards playing the characters as self-sufficent. Not all, but most.<br /> <br /> (Funny enough, kinfolk on the other hand are rarely so defined by their jobs.)<br /> <br /> Given that the MET book also listed jobs and the Rage rules work differently, adding to the general uncomfortability people have for the animal side at times (lupus being shock jockey, garou seeing dogs as only animals,etc) it is very hard to think what would be a good compromise between the Curse and players wanting their characters to not be moochers.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T18:16:04-08:00","id":1166867,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166856#post1166856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> They want their characters to be EMT workers, police officers, doctors and so on. I guess it is also the aging of the playerbase as a factor, too. In the first WtA game I officially played, I got told that my Rage 4 Galliard should find her own apartment and job in the city, garou do not live in communes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wonder how much of this is actually that for many players &quot;occupation equals character concept.&quot; Many PCs start from that, and everything else character-wise is filled in later (to varying success in order to become a well fleshed out character). So if their Garou does not have a real job, they don't know where to begin. It's for that reason I see a lot of &quot;Lost Cubs&quot; concepts - even if they don't work at a job NOW, at least they know previously their character was a nurse, lawyer, CEO, soldier, or whatever.<br /> <br /> That is ironic because Garou actually begin the game with their Garou nation occupation already determined - their auspice. Furthermore, Garou can easily survive without a human job. Just live in the wilderness and hunt deer in lupus form. Garou have job duties and responsibilities to the Nation, and you don't need a sept office to do so. Ragabashes can be scouts over known Wyrm lairs. Ahrouns can be sentries. Theurges should be learning rites, creating talens, and creating ties with spirits. Galliards need to master a huge amount of oral lore and heritage. Philodoxes should be studying the Litany, mediating disputes, and making sure their tribe members are upholding tribal ideals. And that is just generic auspice duties. Packs are supposed to have quests, and pack members should be working with their totems to embody that purpose.<br /> <br /> Garou should have a full time job just being Garou. If a Wyrm menace appears, a spirit needs help, or there's a big argument between septs or packs that could spiral out of control, what does the Garou say to explain they can't make it because they have to get to work on time for their 9-5 job?<br /> <br /> Now this can be taken too far. It's possible for a Garou character to be so removed from human society, that they become a fantasy adventurer that seems to live in a different world entirely than the actual setting. This is why it can be beneficial to include other elements that help bind them to the real world - you can use the natural environment, ordinary animals as NPCs (although these too can be affected by the Curse, but various Gifts can help overcome that), and even spirits to ground them. And the occasional high Willpower human or kinfolk (knowing or unknowing) NPC can do the same.<br /> <br /> I can understand why STs may be concerned that not having a job makes Garou too much like fantasy adventurers, and they want the PC to have ties to the human world. But that is exactly the function of kinfolk!<br /> <br /> The PC I am playing now is a high school drop out who lives in the backyard camper trailer of his extended (knowing) kinfolk family. A buddy's PC (an ahroun) was disowned by his family because of his &quot;emotional problems&quot; which caused him to be ejected out of multiple schools (the last one was a military school he was sent to in order to install discipline) and is now squatting in an abandoned hunter's shack in the woods, but he still gets hand outs from his family secretly because they (unknowing kinfolk) all know the stories of their family's history of &quot;mental illness&quot;. Another is a trust fund child who has bought a wilderness estate out of inherited funds, who quit his old job because of a &quot;nervous breakdown&quot; and his inability to lead a &quot;real life&quot; causes problems with his parents and siblings. Despite none of these three characters having a job, we all have plenty to do in interacting with the human world through our kinfolk and neighbors, and focus to varying degrees on Garou issues. One low Rage PC does have a job, but he basically rents out some stuff and have other people work it. But for the most part we're recluses who live on the fringe of society, but we do have strong human relationships through our kinfolk.<br /> <br /> In some of these cases, the PCs did not bother to buy Kinfolk. So they don't get control of relatives in game, but the ST still roleplays their relationships out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166856#post1166856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given that the MET book also listed jobs and the Rage rules work differently, adding to the general uncomfortability people have for the animal side at times (lupus being shock jockey, garou seeing dogs as only animals,etc) it is very hard to think what would be a good compromise between the Curse and players wanting their characters to not be moochers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the first time they save a family member from not being vampirized or become fomori food, the Garou will have earned their keep.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T18:21:57-08:00","id":1166869,"title":"","content":"There's also another issue I noticed when one of my buddies played in a Werewolf LARP. After wtaching the other players for a game session he turned to me and said, &quot;Their problem is that they think they are playing Vampire.&quot; Some players just don't understand the dynamic is very different. The creatures are different, and the tropes are different even though both vampires and werewolves are part of the horror genre.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T19:22:39-08:00","id":1166884,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166867#post1166867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the first time they save a family member from not being vampirized or become fomori food, the Garou will have earned their keep.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is in-universe, I think most players look at things like that from a more broad view, looking at how the garou handle situations wholesale.<br /> <br /> <br /> From your post,though, I do think the issue is the 'playing vampire' part. Or, more accurately, playing the person. I do think that you need to go a bit more in the fantasy direction with your wants, if you want to play garou as more spiritual beings. Because often the main focus is on the characters as people, and as such, what connections to other humans they have.<br /> <br /> Lacking those connections or the ability to have them, seems to be a red flag for many. I asked in non-WtA rps about the situation, and the general idea seemed to be that those human connections are valued unless the game itself is interesting.<br /> <br /> Mind, nothing stopped the kinfolk pcs from being the investigators and the garou from focusing on garou stuff, nobody just did it that way XD<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T20:57:15-08:00","id":1166898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties#post1166651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This brings to mind one question, is the Curse something that you guys would miss if it was removed? For me, I would like it to be used, but often my fellow players would not agree with this. The mentality I have seen is geared to garou that more often are more likely to do a lot of grunt work themselves and are less likely just to focus on spiritualism and fighting the Wyrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Having been part of those 24/7 ish games. I will say, thats mostly true because fighting the wyrm is boring. Not saying I hate that aspect as it informs the relationships of the faction and the setting itself but the fighting itself, unless handles correctly, turns into killing truckloads of BSDs named &quot;Skull fuck death rape&quot; or any combination of those. While fighting the wyrm the slow way via improving the surrounding require Garou to interact with people.<br /> <br /> Fighting 24/7 in the sept can be fun for some but for other isnt. Personally i played a PC that came outside of the sept and joined (as i didnt knew anything about the city we were playing in), played a Philodox and took no points in kinfolk but never raised my rage. Had a somewhat open hours job. The DM made a point of making plenty of location being owned of keep by Kinfolks so they never felt they were useless but almost all of the PC had a job. Social worker in women shelter for my PC, oddjobs for another, programmer (that one had rage 9), farmer, etc.<br /> <br /> In that game the kinfolk were a big focus, on how they live and what their ties were to the society and Garou one. There was even a Kinfolk who was technically wyrmfoe (a mentally challenge metis was the official holder of the position but he managed everything)<br /> <br /> The way the DM handle it was that Rage wasnt an inmediately &quot;everyone runs&quot; but that only affected on the long term. You could be the super chill rage 9 character and interact with humans for a scene or two but constant contact would trigger rage.<br /> <br /> Personally in a home game i would treat rage same way i treat gay Garous. Ask the player if what they want is just play high rage garou because of the mechanics and thats it, we just pretend isnt there and dont call attention to it. Or if they wanna play the full extend of the social consequences of Rage, and accomodate to that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T23:57:30-08:00","id":1166924,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166898#post1166898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Having been part of those 24/7 ish games. I will say, thats mostly true because fighting the wyrm is boring. Not saying I hate that aspect as it informs the relationships of the faction and the setting itself but the fighting itself, unless handles correctly, turns into killing truckloads of BSDs named &quot;Skull fuck death rape&quot; or any combination of those. While fighting the wyrm the slow way via improving the surrounding require Garou to interact with people.<br /> <br /> Fighting 24/7 in the sept can be fun for some but for other isnt. Personally i played a PC that came outside of the sept and joined (as i didnt knew anything about the city we were playing in), played a Philodox and took no points in kinfolk but never raised my rage. Had a somewhat open hours job. The DM made a point of making plenty of location being owned of keep by Kinfolks so they never felt they were useless but almost all of the PC had a job. Social worker in women shelter for my PC, oddjobs for another, programmer (that one had rage 9), farmer, etc.<br /> <br /> In that game the kinfolk were a big focus, on how they live and what their ties were to the society and Garou one. There was even a Kinfolk who was technically wyrmfoe (a mentally challenge metis was the official holder of the position but he managed everything)<br /> <br /> The way the DM handle it was that Rage wasnt an inmediately &quot;everyone runs&quot; but that only affected on the long term. You could be the super chill rage 9 character and interact with humans for a scene or two but constant contact would trigger rage.<br /> <br /> Personally in a home game i would treat rage same way i treat gay Garous. Ask the player if what they want is just play high rage garou because of the mechanics and thats it, we just pretend isnt there and dont call attention to it. Or if they wanna play the full extend of the social consequences of Rage, and accomodate to that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I agree that constantly fighting the Wyrm is boring in a forum game XD<br /> <br /> <br /> If the kinfolk actually did something and weren't just there to look pretty and be breeding stock,cool. Though it could have been cool to bring plots from the garou -trying- that and the kinfolk having to babysit them. Instead of the typical 'who is whose mate and who claims whom' that can often happen with kinfolk pcs.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-12T05:14:48-08:00","id":1166958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166924#post1166924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the kinfolk actually did something and weren't just there to look pretty and be breeding stock,cool. Though it could have been cool to bring plots from the garou -trying- that and the kinfolk having to babysit them. Instead of the typical 'who is whose mate and who claims whom' that can often happen with kinfolk pcs.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you are the DM then the only way to make kinfolks interesting is to showcase how interesting they could be. In the game I was, the kinfolk had an entire subculture of their own in the nation. Also, PC could have their Garous and also play their kinfolks.<br /> <br /> You had a kinfolk whose &quot;official&quot; job was to be breeding stock of the highest purebreed for the most influential family on the sept. And he was also one of the most interesting characters showcasing a person who can pass off as normal but was thoroughly raised into the Garou nation and the Shadowlords from birth and the expectative that carried.<br /> <br /> Mother, daughter, Sons, Fathers, friends, and cousins all can be kinfolks as alternatives to &quot;mates&quot;. My character had a kinfolk mater, he didn't start with one but got her in play as she was established NPC. But aside from being his mate (which was a whole campaign worth of effort), she was the mechanic of the Sept, the person made cars disappear, the former mate of another kinfolk and the cousin of the Sept alpha who was the only one to be able to fight with her as they were raised together.<br /> <br /> Kinfolks can fill spots that kinda require &quot; an NPC with an exclamation point to stand still in a location&quot;. Say the father of one of the kinfolks and a kinfolk friend from one of the Garou in the sept both had gyms (one boxing the other parkour) so if you wanted to raise brawl or athletics you went there as the place would accommodate Garou needs. PCs might never interact with the kinfolk owners or they might.<br /> <br /> Want an education but the Garou hours arent university friendly? Then one of the kinfolks had that position in the university and she would see that you can continue and justify your absences and would assign you a kinfolk to accompany in campus to help you in case you have an &quot;accident&quot;<br /> <br /> Want a house to live in? The local head of the Glasswalker (who is also a kinfolk) owned lots of properties (having garous to back your business interests is useful) can rent you one.<br /> <br /> And those are practical concerns, then there the whole subculture angle. Using the example of the game, the kinfolks there were many childhood friends or at least known from the established families. They had things like a mini league of tennis which the Garou weren't invited because if they did &quot;they had to let them win&quot;, it was nothing official or super secretive but more like an inside joke among them.<br /> <br /> Or the players (playing their kinfolks) organized a D&amp;D night and the whole scene was them playing their kinfolk and talking.<br /> <br /> Even plots can revolve around the kinfolks and use them to include the Garou PCs.<br /> Example: the local GW company had a race team and were competing in a local race and one of the competing teams was pentex subsidiary who were cheating. The race itself was important for the kinfolks themselves (not in the grand scheme of things) as they all cooperate to make it happen. So the PC got involved without stealing the kinfolks show by helping from the sidelines (also because it was kind of illegal to switch drivers in the middle of a race that way). The PCs all got behind that with more investment than fighting a hive, because they knew the people involved and they wanted to help them.<br /> <br /> Also, don't fear to showcase different personalities to the kinfolks. Not all of them are submissive, most kinfolks raised in the nation are like slaves, they know who is their &quot;owner&quot; but also know how much safely they can push their leash. The Kinfolk of my PC was had a dismissive attitude to most Garou edgy but never crossing the disrespectful safe in the knowledge that her connections to both of the most powerful Kinfolk families meant that as long as she never outright insulted a Garou she was untouchable and any Garou who tried would bear the anger of the Sept alpha and an elder.<br /> <br /> Even if just about mating, have an NPC kinfolk (normally a sister/brother or even mother/father) explain to them how the systems works. Many PCs also play the equalitarian Garous who treat kinfolks and Garou the same, in the name of fairness.<br /> My PC was one of those (he was a lost cub) so then a kinfolk calmly explain to him that he was &quot;one of those Garou&quot; (and that phrase by itself does wonders to showcase the inner culture of the kinfolk btw), that he was dangerous because he didn't get that the rules are also there to protect kinfolks and them pushing them aside put the kinfolks in danger. That when a Kinfolk goes to his &quot;owner&quot; to ask permission to mate with a Garou sometimes is that oppression is real but other times is because if the kinfolk doesn't want to be with the Garou, the kinfolk can deflect the anger of rejection to the Garou owner. That telling &quot;no, I don't want you as a partner&quot; is one of the things that most hurt one\u00b4s ego and telling that to a person with anger issues, who can transform into a giant monster and rip you apart and whose life in the society you live it is more valuable is a scary and dangerous proposition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 12-12-2017, 08:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-12T05:22:17-08:00","id":1166959,"title":"","content":"I've used troupe style play for my chronicles so if players wanted to focus on the human interaction side they could use some of the Kinfolk characters for a while. It gave a nice change of pace to the game as well.<br /> <br /> LokiRavenSpeak's post has me thinking about a multi generational chronicle of Gurahl Kin revolving around the periodic awakening of a Gurahl.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Lead Writer for the Savage Age of Werewolf: the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/428750/Ghost-Lineages-The-Advanced-Players-Guide-to-the-Savage-Age\" target=\"_blank\">Ghost Lineages</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/312700/The-Rise-of-the-Garou-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Two\" target=\"_blank\">Rise of the Garou</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/331302/Tools-of-Extinction-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Three\" target=\"_blank\">Tools of Extinction</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/352717/Tribebook-Wlewa\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook: Wlewa</a>.<br /> <br /> Want RPGs in libraries? Check out my book, <a href=\"http://www.abc-clio.com/ABC-CLIOCorporate/product.aspx?pc=A3951P\" target=\"_blank\">Dragons in the Stacks</a>. Now with <a href=\"http://www.ennie-awards.com/blog/2015-ennie-award-nominees-and-spotlight-winners/\" target=\"_blank\">awards</a>!<br /> \u200b","edited":{"userid":1813,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","user":"grimjaws","date":". 12-12-2017, 10:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","name":"grimjaws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1813_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-15T10:08:30-08:00","id":1167948,"title":"","content":"How the Curse is handled is one of my main complaints with how the game is commonly played. I\u2019m currently playing a Metis Ahroun, his job in his birth Sept was blacksmithing and maintaining weapons for defense. The ST dropped our pack into a town and expects my character to interact with the townsfolk. I tried to explain how that won\u2019t really work for my character and most people will go out of their way to clear the area any time he shows up. Instead the nice little old lady next door tries to give him cookies. Sure, that may work for our Theurge and Ragabash, but the massive Get Ahroun? I took almost no social skills to reflect how a socially stunted and abused individual would have major issues interacting with anyone, let alone society at large.<br /> <br /> Kinfolk are a great way to get a pack of Garou interacting with NPCs and gives them an emotional investment in the game. Not everyone in a pack can interact well with humans and even those that can will be colored by their association with that weirdo everyone knows must secretly be a murderer or thug. Trying to role play the Curse or get others to do so is difficult because not all players or even STs are aware of how big an impact it should have on the daily lives of the Garou.<br /> <br /> The Garou are monsters, monsters trying to save the world, but monsters nonetheless. The Curse helps prevent the stereotype of furry superheroes. It makes players think about the requirement of cultivating working relationships with Kin. Garou may be incredibly powerful in many regards, but the Curse takes some of that away and presents new and unique challenges to take on during play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":3198,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3198-lunar-falcon","user":"Lunar Falcon","date":". 12-15-2017, 01:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3198-lunar-falcon","name":"Lunar Falcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3198_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-15T22:17:42-08:00","id":1168127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lunar Falcon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1167948#post1167948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How the Curse is handled is one of my main complaints with how the game is commonly played. I\u2019m currently playing a Metis Ahroun, his job in his birth Sept was blacksmithing and maintaining weapons for defense. The ST dropped our pack into a town and expects my character to interact with the townsfolk. I tried to explain how that won\u2019t really work for my character and most people will go out of their way to clear the area any time he shows up. Instead the nice little old lady next door tries to give him cookies. Sure, that may work for our Theurge and Ragabash, but the massive Get Ahroun? I took almost no social skills to reflect how a socially stunted and abused individual would have major issues interacting with anyone, let alone society at large.</div> </div> </div> </div>Granted, you play the game the ST creates, so if in that version of WoD the Rage isn't an issue then it isn't. But still, if the play is going to be human social focused, then the ST should have noted your character might not fit in the game.<br /> <br /> Another thing is that a metis might just be a Veil breach not just by looks, but how they act. Kinfolk would be the only humans they'd have a decent relationship with, and even then it could be coloured with prejudice from the kin OR from the metis. An abused metis in the town is pretty lost, though I'd suggest you'd try to go to a hardware store or look for a human blacksmith to make the trip worth it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-16T08:21:08-08:00","id":1168193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168127#post1168127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Granted, you play the game the ST creates, so if in that version of WoD the Rage isn't an issue then it isn't. But still, if the play is going to be human social focused, then the ST should have noted your character might not fit in the game.<br /> <br /> Another thing is that a metis might just be a Veil breach not just by looks, but how they act. Kinfolk would be the only humans they'd have a decent relationship with, and even then it could be coloured with prejudice from the kin OR from the metis. An abused metis in the town is pretty lost, though I'd suggest you'd try to go to a hardware store or look for a human blacksmith to make the trip worth it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good perspective and great ideas. Rather than saying 'my character can't do this, bad adventure,' with a bit of a perspective change this becomes a rare chance to explore the world. Maybe the malformed smith is a potential Veil breach, but at the same time, a Metis could easily jump at the chance to go off the ranch. Yes, he's a fish out of water, but that doesn't sound like an inherently bad thing. Even if the character is bad at connecting with people and things go sideways, that is still an adventure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-16T16:07:29-08:00","id":1168265,"title":"","content":"I haven\u2019t told the ST that it\u2019s a bad adventure or anything. Just pointed out that most people in town would actively try to avoid him so the ST expecting him to go and be buddies with a lot of humans shouldn\u2019t get their hopes up. That and his deformity is reflecting Crinos in all forms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3198-lunar-falcon","name":"Lunar Falcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3198_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-16T22:08:37-08:00","id":1168311,"title":"","content":"on the curse?<br /> <br /> ive wondered a bit, how would it work when 'mark of the wolf' is used on someone, how will it disrupt friendships and family relationships? for someone people thought they knew to suddenly give off a creepy vibe?<br /> <br /> or with 'bury the wolf' someone people allready categorized mentally as 'weird' or strange. coming around smiling disarmingly?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-17T04:26:43-08:00","id":1168346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lunar Falcon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168265#post1168265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven\u2019t told the ST that it\u2019s a bad adventure or anything. Just pointed out that most people in town would actively try to avoid him so the ST expecting him to go and be buddies with a lot of humans shouldn\u2019t get their hopes up. That and his deformity is reflecting Crinos in all forms.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oooh, yikes. Yeah, I understand your worry over it, that deformity is pretty much a huge Veil breach unless your metis wears a fullbody cloack or something to hide the wolf shape.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168311#post1168311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">on the curse?<br /> <br /> ive wondered a bit, how would it work when 'mark of the wolf' is used on someone, how will it disrupt friendships and family relationships? for someone people thought they knew to suddenly give off a creepy vibe?<br /> <br /> or with 'bury the wolf' someone people allready categorized mentally as 'weird' or strange. coming around smiling disarmingly?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, the always have had the creepy vibe since entering teens.<br /> <br /> For the wolf burying, I think the person feels like they got 'off' from their troubled life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-17T06:47:37-08:00","id":1168358,"title":"","content":"hmmnn, how do high social attributes and abilitys, persuasion, and 'the curse' interact???","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-17T20:23:16-08:00","id":1168467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168358#post1168358\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">hmmnn, how do high social attributes and abilitys, persuasion, and 'the curse' interact???</div> </div> </div> </div>By making your character seem more intense than frightening. Except for Intimidation, which acts more as a focus for Rage, given that if you don't have a high enough Charisma + Intimidation, you have to roll Rage instead, and risk losing the contest due to frenzy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T04:25:29-08:00","id":1168501,"title":"","content":"I found it disturbing, that its valid for a Garou to learn intimidation, just to reduce the number of rage roll's they make.<br /> <br /> otherwise, putting an upstart kin in their place requires a rage roll.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T08:58:42-08:00","id":1168537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168501#post1168501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I found it disturbing, that its valid for a Garou to learn intimidation, just to reduce the number of rage roll's they make.<br /> <br /> otherwise, putting an upstart kin in their place requires a rage roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really. Staredowns are mostly against other Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T15:39:02-08:00","id":1168652,"title":"","content":"Isnt just giving a glance to a child to 'behave' using intimidate?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T16:19:34-08:00","id":1168663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168652#post1168652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isnt just giving a glance to a child to 'behave' using intimidate?</div> </div> </div> </div>But not necessarily, or usually, a staredown as it is mechanically defined.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T16:24:47-08:00","id":1168667,"title":"","content":"It's kinda up to the ST?<br /> <br /> That could be a use of the Intimidation Ability, or Leadership, or etc.<br /> <br /> Threatening someone into compliance is certainly Intimidation, but shaming someone into compliance, etc. could easily be other Abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T17:01:06-08:00","id":1168674,"title":"","content":"but ANY use of intimidate could use rage way rules are written.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-19T11:52:29-08:00","id":1168869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1168674#post1168674\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but ANY use of intimidate could use rage way rules are written.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most often, if you go that route, it's because you don't need to roll, because Rage automatically makes others afraid of you if you look at them funny.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-19T23:35:48-08:00","id":1169018,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't bother to even roll to have a werewolf intimidate a child. I couldn't think of a more trivial challenge... unless the kid is made of really stern stuff for some particular reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T00:13:03-08:00","id":1169025,"title":"","content":"It does seem a trivial challenge, but if you have a high Rage character (such that they'd have to roll Rage instead) the odds are good that they'd have a decent risk of Frenzy. So there's at least the drama in &quot;scare the child into compliance, or deal with the possibility of Frenzy,&quot; that could make it worth rolling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T00:45:32-08:00","id":1169026,"title":"","content":"I've never had people roll for making someone afraid vs making someone afraid and getting what they want I mean intimidating a child into shutting up could just as readily push them into crying out of fear for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T02:46:15-08:00","id":1169036,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1166811#post1166811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Curse is very important and emphasize it in my chronicles. It's one of the few things in the game that helps bolt the setting into the typical horror movies and stories we expect in the World of Darkness as opposed to the high fantasy adventure the game can too easily become.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Definitely this. I remember my first werewolf game. I run it. And I remember how my players (mostly D&amp;D players) were sure as hell excited about &quot;oh wow we get to play werewolves in this game, sounds awesome&quot; but when I explained how the Curse works... well, living as Garou suddenly sounded less awesome. Even players that usually aren't much invested about their characters genuinely felt bad for them. The Curse sucks, and so it's great. You're a monster and people around you feel it.<br /> <br /> As you said in another post, it's not Vampire but many players want to play WtA in a similar way. And I can get why. Vampire has generally more appeal for a bigger audience. The fact that in a vampire game you have many occassions to play many scenes where you pose as an &quot;average human, but with a twist&quot; while your surroundings stay fairly mundane, I'm sure that's a big draw for a lot of the audience.<br /> <br /> Also I understand that people playing WtA 24/7 want some other stuff to do and want to be more immersed into their alter egos and probably having a dayjob helps them feel their characters more real because they become more &quot;human&quot;, more &quot;normal&quot;.<br /> <br /> The recent poll about what races are the most played among the current D&amp;D players tell us that the most played race is human. I'm for the more escapist side of fantasy, so it really puzzled me how the plain human was the more favoured choice. But if we take into account the desire of losing one-self into their alter-ego I can get it. I don't share the desire, but I get it.<br /> It's more normal, more understandable, more easy to *become* that person.<br /> And also yeah, having a dayjob makes the character more believable. The occupation, the role in human society is an important building block for a believable human character.<br /> <br /> But hey, while I can get why some players like that I personally dislike it.<br /> It's WtA and for me it's about being a monster. Heroic as only the more tragic heroes can be, but still a monster. Sure, it's an awesome and cool monster (let's admit it please) but also it sucks greatly being one, and there is no escape. Because as a Garou you're shunned by everyone else. You're shunned by human and wolf society. You're not welcome. Yet you're tasked with saving everyone else because some old wolf geezer said you so. Because if you won't do it no one will do it. And yet people will hate and fear you while you're trying to save their butt (funnily this aspect of it sounds much like X-men). The stakes are the highest possible: the world is dying and who's in charge isn't doing shit about it (sounds familiar?). From a Garou perspective mankind is in charge of the world now, and humans are only doing more harm.<br /> And it boils down to the question: when will you rage?<br /> When will you be so fed up of everything that you will just go out on a killing spree? Or join the wyrm because to hell with everything? Or embrace red talons ideology and begin human extermination? But you don't want to do those things, they're terrible things. So when will it be that your rage and your moral restraints clash? Will you go crazy?<br /> Will you go mad by rage?<br /> The alternative is to Lose the Wolf permanently. You can give up, no problem. Who cares about the world?<br /> I like the Curse, it adds another factor to the maddening rage-fueled existence of this human-beasts hybrids that I love.<br /> Seriously a lot of stuff of the setting seems to be designed to make Garou existence maddening. Wolves and humans fear you and will forever do it, but you can't mate with other Garou. You were never human, you just thought you were. Yet you must coexist with them and protect them and absolutely not kill them because your religion says so. Rage is good, but also bad. The moon is good, but also bad. The Garou Nation is made of allies, but also isn't. The Nation could win the war, but it's too busy bickering with each other. Avoiding Apocalypse is hopeless, yet it is THE good fight so it's worth fighting... but you're left with asking yourself what's the point.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong. I think it's great. But I also think it's the point of the game. If some people like something different it's fine.<br /> It was just my rant about why aspects like the Curse or the ecoterrorists or the &quot;captain planet with furries&quot; aspects of the game should stay.<br /> And I'll also add that they don't need to be much nuanced. Sometimes simple and straightforward works the best. Ok maybe not as simple as &quot;captain planet with furries&quot;, but you got it.<br /> <br /> Also for me that I'm able to play only 2 or 3 sessions per month of any RPG... Well, the idea of having to lose precious session time for playing out dayjobs or even having to deal with them for a few minutes... Let's say I'd immediately make a rage roll, but not as a character ahahah <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T06:12:25-08:00","id":1169065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1169036#post1169036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This. Definitely this. I remember my first werewolf game. I run it. And I remember how my players (mostly D&amp;D players) were sure as hell excited about &quot;oh wow we get to play werewolves in this game, sounds awesome&quot; but when I explained how the Curse works... well, living as Garou suddenly sounded less awesome. Even players that usually aren't much invested about their characters genuinely felt bad for them. The Curse sucks, and so it's great. You're a monster and people around you feel it.<br /> <br /> As you said in another post, it's not Vampire but many players want to play WtA in a similar way. And I can get why. Vampire has generally more appeal for a bigger audience. The fact that in a vampire game you have many occassions to play many scenes where you pose as an &quot;average human, but with a twist&quot; while your surroundings stay fairly mundane, I'm sure that's a big draw for a lot of the audience.<br /> <br /> Also I understand that people playing WtA 24/7 want some other stuff to do and want to be more immersed into their alter egos and probably having a dayjob helps them feel their characters more real because they become more &quot;human&quot;, more &quot;normal&quot;.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I don't think Vampire promotes having a mundane job vs being a mooch/leach because of a combination of the kindred condition and rather easy way to get around having to deal with basics of human society. There's even more of a carrot for Vampires to go &quot;fuck this I'm a vampire first&quot; with Ghouls which a vampire will have near complete control over vs Kinfolk who garou can't replace nor have that level of devotion. This is before the fact that your average vampire trying to hold down a job is going to freak out and murder someone given enough time, or get called in during the day etc.<br /> <br /> I don't see how ignoring the curse is playing the game more like Vampire vs... games where they allow that level of open activity being generally against the setup but promoting a more &quot;superheroic&quot; feel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T08:38:54-08:00","id":1169097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1169026#post1169026\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've never had people roll for making someone afraid vs making someone afraid and getting what they want I mean intimidating a child into shutting up could just as readily push them into crying out of fear for example.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Its almost a forgone conclusion, a garou would spend rage to cancel a frenzy in such circumstances?<br /> <br /> does the Mercy gift, make it that muchless imperative to do so? <br /> <br /> I think cases like this are where Kinfolk Nana's come from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-20T14:42:33-08:00","id":1169203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1166651-rage-and-the-curse-versus-human-ties?p=1169065#post1169065\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I don't think Vampire promotes having a mundane job vs being a mooch/leach because of a combination of the kindred condition and rather easy way to get around having to deal with basics of human society. There's even more of a carrot for Vampires to go &quot;fuck this I'm a vampire first&quot; with Ghouls which a vampire will have near complete control over vs Kinfolk who garou can't replace nor have that level of devotion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said that vampire promotes having a dayjob. But the game surely gives the idea that more mundane situations are more common in a vampire game, like going to clubs, theatre, museums, drug dealing in a alley, meeting your contact in a bar. Sure it can happen in a werewolf game too, but with a lot of wilderness, umbra, sept meetings, caerns etc. the game gives the idea the mundane situations of modern-day-life are less common. Of course it can just be a misconception given some vampire games I was in, but the fact that often the unit of setting the chronicle is built around is a modern mundane city it's something that matters.<br /> Giving your werewolf a dayjob is a simple and straightforward way to have your character connected and immersed in the mundane world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is before the fact that your average vampire trying to hold down a job is going to freak out and murder someone given enough time, or get called in during the day etc. </div> </div> </div>aside from the sunlight thing, how your average werewolf is different in this?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't see how ignoring the curse is playing the game more like Vampire vs... games where they allow that level of open activity being generally against the setup but promoting a more &quot;superheroic&quot; feel. </div> </div> </div>if you ignore the Curse mundane situations are more easily resolved with social interaction. It makes the game more similar to vampire for the reasons I explained above. Of course it's just my personal view about it, my hypothesis about one of the reasons (but there are many and I have many hypothesis) of why the larger audience prefers Vampire <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I'm not sure if I'm getting the exact meaning of the second sentence. What do you mean by open activity? Dayjob? Mundane situations? They are allowed or they are against the setup? vampire and werewolf give a superheroic feel? I'm sorry but I'm having a bit of hard time to get this one sentence <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-22T19:51:24-08:00","id":1169804,"title":"","content":"To everyone who commented on the Curse - can I just say that I want to thank you all for making me actually like Apocalypse? To be exact, dispelling me of some... notions I had of the game and WoD in general? I won\u2019t be Storytelling or playing, at least in the foreseen future, since I\u2019m busy and all, but still.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-29T07:32:10-08:00","id":1171528,"title":"","content":"in changing ways, causing the curse is one of the uses of primal urge.<br /> <br /> in what ways, is causing the curse beneficial?<br /> <br /> it causes people to &quot;leave me alone&quot; ?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-29T10:40:46-08:00","id":1171564,"title":"","content":"If you don't have great social stats, purposefully inflicting the Curse is a great way to clear an area of normal folks and avoid breaking the Veil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}